> Each turn of the machine's main wheel stands for a year, and the gears give the Moon's pointer 254 turns in 19 years. That ratio is a very little too slow against the stars. Since 205 BC it has dropped the pointer so far behind the Moon, most of the way round the dial, that the pointer now stands ahead of it, as a clock that has lost nine hours shows three hours fast: on 23 November 2026, 105° ahead, nearly a third of a turn (in the picture, ahead is anticlockwise). The slow running has cost most of one turn in nearly thirty thousand. It spoiled the pointer early, all the same: the machine's model of how the Moon moves was otherwise good to about a degree, and the slow running added up to more than a degree within nine years.
